A||N||9||Haze_Performance||6.5" Bazooka amplified  tubes?? ||||10||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||04-09-2001||02:16 AM||||<I>I was thinking of buying two Bazooka RS Series RS6A-HP Amplified Tubes. Crutchfield #204RS6AHP $299.95 each.</I><P><B>Features:<BR> <BR>power output 150 watts RMS <BR>50-250 Hz variable low-pass filter, 18 dB/octave <BR>frequency response 39-250 Hz <BR>impedance 2 ohms <BR>sensitivity 100 dB <BR>19-1/8"L x 7"W x 8-3/8"H</B> <P><I>My new Fiero is a 5-speed.. I pull the seat up alittle. I would like to put a tube behind each seat.<P>Whats the Pro's & Con's? Anyone ever use 2 amped tubes? Hows it sound?</I><P><BR><B>TIA<BR>Haze   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR></B><BR>    <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">   </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||stimpy||04-09-2001||09:07 AM||||Yikes! $600 plus tax! Whoo. OK, here is my experiences. I had a Sony 8" amplified tube (which worked great, but was stolen from me). Sounded good, but not as much crisp snap as my Polk sub. Had a 6.5" non-amplified Bazooka tube running off of a cheaper 200w amp. Very muddy muffled sound. Heard a guys stereo who had an 8" bazooka running off an old-school 500w Altec-Lansing amp. It would shake yor fillings, but it also took up the majority of the passenger leg space.<BR>For $600 you could get one of my footwell enclosures (shameless pug) with a Polk Momo 10" driver and a VERY good amp, and probably still have change left. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-09-2001||10:52 AM||||I had one 6.5 EL powered sub behind the passenger seat. Sounded good. 2 would sound better. They are easy to hook up. But you may be better off with with buying the non-powered and getting an amp. Or waiting until next week when I post the "how to fit an 8-inch sub in the stock location" complete with drwings of what each piece of the box is. At the very least, buy them from here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/product.asp?sku=10818" TARGET=_blank>http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/product.asp?sku=10818</A> <P>They are less expensive than Crutchfield. On the ones you want, you'll save $60.00 for the pair!||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||04-09-2001||12:51 PM||||Bazooka tubes are over-rated and very expensive. and I have yet to hear one that sounds any good. Get a real sub, a truckbox for behind the seat or a Stimpy footwell box,and an amp and then you'll have some real bass.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Haze_Performance||04-09-2001||03:59 PM||||Thanks for the comments guys........Oreif Ill be waiting for your 8" sub plans. <P><BR>The Bazooka amplified tubes were my first idea.. (It seemed easier that my 2nd)<P>2nd Idea:<P>2 10" subs in the rear firewall<P><BR>Remove (cut out) the panels on the rear firewall. (I get about 3" more room behind the seats) Install sound proofing & heat wrap on the rear wall (inside hole) Insert a pre-made single 10" sealed box in each hole. (Ill only lose about 3" to 4" of space behind each seat) Mount the boxes, fill in & recarpet the entire rear firewall. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">   </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Oreif||04-09-2001||06:31 PM||||Haze, Hopefully by Friday you'll be able to see the sub box! I am working on it this week. I took all the measurements and I can fit a nice size box there. I just have to move my "blue dingy thingy". An 8" sub requires .3 cu ft of volume and I can squeeze a .39 cu ft box there. So there are many brands of subs that will work within that volume.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||04-09-2001||09:24 PM||||Go to a decent stereo shop and demo a Bazooka in your car. The shop here does it. They'll bring it out to the parking lot and hook it up to your system. I had a single non-powered 6 in the Lambo clone and one in a Fiero. Both mounted on the passenger side floor. I was very happy with them. I had an 8 in a pickup, sounded good. I now have two sixes behind the seat of my truck and I don't really like the sound of them in there. Gotta work on that. Bazookas need to be corner loaded, it makes a huge difference how you have them pointed/mounted. Certain configurations just don't work in some cars. Anyway, the demo is a great way to see how it will sound in YOUR car. I only paid $99 for a non-powered 6. The powered ones use an integrated circuit amp, not the greatest. Better to use a separate amp.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||04-10-2001||06:14 AM||||Oreif had a 6 1/2" EL powered Tube.  Now I have it.  I don't think it sounds muddy at all.  Probably not as clean a sound as a "real" sub and amp, but still sounds damn good.  Thanx again, Oreif.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||04-10-2001||04:17 PM||||Right on MM! My 6.5 EL was actually very punchy.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Haze_Performance||04-10-2001||05:11 PM||||Hmmmmmm.. It looks like tubes isnt the way to go huh? I had a VERY lage system in one of my others cars.. ( I miss it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) Im looking to get some BOOM!! back in my life.<P>OK now NO rice or Hyundai jokes!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1994HyundiExcel.jpg"> <P>I guess if I really want to make some bass in my Fiero, Ill need to do a Fierwall setup..<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">  </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A>||||reg||10
